        "children": [
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "id": "9t3v34enbg3k5cc",
                "text": "Defining and Measuring Acids and Bases: Summary"
              }
            ],
            "id": "bps27q3ajne6ytp",
            "type": "h1"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "A compound that can donate a proton (a hydrogen ion) to another compound is called a Brønsted-Lowry acid. The compound that accepts the proton is called a Brønsted-Lowry base. The species remaining after a Brønsted-Lowry acid has lost a proton is the conjugate base of the acid. The species formed when a Brønsted-Lowry base gains a proton is the conjugate acid of the base. An acid-base reaction occurs when a proton is transferred from an acid to a base, with formation of the conjugate base of the reactant acid and formation of the conjugate acid of the reactant base. Amphiprotic or amphoteric species can act as both proton donors and proton acceptors. Water is the most important amphiprotic species. It can form both the hydronium ion, H"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "3"
              },
              {
                "text": "O"
              },
              {
                "sup": true,
                "text": "+"
              },
              {
                "text": ", and the hydroxide ion, OH"
              },
              {
                "sup": true,
                "text": "−"
              },
              {
                "text": " when it undergoes autoionization:"
              }
            ],
            "id": "0fkw7hzysqjwcdz",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "id": "eu2sv8z0505c3bc",
                "text": " ",
                "type": "text"
              }
            ],
            "id": "bip6xs96ior344q",
            "legacyBlockRendered": "true",
            "src": "{\\mathrm H}_2\\mathrm O(l)\\;\\;\\rightleftharpoons\\;\\;\\mathrm H^+(aq)\\;+\\;\\mathrm{OH}^-(aq)",
            "subtype": "latex",
            "type": "formula"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "The ion product of water, "
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "text": "K"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "w"
              },
              {
                "text": " is the equilibrium constant for the autoionization reaction:"
              }
            ],
            "id": "5a07n6ub9p0nrfo",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "id": "hlkqdgjio9glk40",
                "text": " ",
                "type": "text"
              }
            ],
            "id": "jorhg8lfgpy6vod",
            "legacyBlockRendered": "true",
            "src": "K_{\\mathrm w}\\;=\\;\\lbrack{\\mathrm H}_3\\mathrm O^+\\rbrack\\lbrack\\mathrm{OH}^-\\rbrack\\;=\\;1.0\\times10^{-14}\\;\\;\\;\\; \\mathrm{at}\\;\\;25^\\circ\\mathrm C",
            "subtype": "latex",
            "type": "formula"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "The concentration of hydronium ion in a solution of an acid in water is greater than 1.0×10"
              },
              {
                "sup": true,
                "text": "−7"
              },
              {
                "text": " M at 25 °C. The concentration of hydroxide ion in a solution of a base in water is greater than 1.0×10"
              },
              {
                "sup": true,
                "text": "−7"
              },
              {
                "text": " M at 25 °C. The concentration of H"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "3"
              },
              {
                "text": "O"
              },
              {
                "sup": true,
                "text": "+"
              },
              {
                "text": " in a solution can be expressed as the pH of the solution; pH = −log [H"
              },
              {
                "sup": true,
                "text": "+"
              },
              {
                "text": "]. The concentration of OH"
              },
              {
                "sup": true,
                "text": "−"
              },
              {
                "text": " can be expressed as the pOH of the solution: pOH = −log [OH"
              },
              {
                "sup": true,
                "text": "−"
              },
              {
                "text": "]. In pure water, pH = 7.00 and pOH = 7.00"
              }
            ],
            "id": "uz5gmapf4203ig3",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "id": "hjm48m34iqj6wtv",
                "text": "End of Lesson Self-Assessment"
              }
            ],
            "id": "dl1vaohszi1njib",
            "type": "h1"
          },
          {
